WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:06.960
There's no shortage of graffiti in most downtowns,&nbsp;
from simple tags to intricate murals. It can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:12.960
found in almost any alley, underpass, and subway&nbsp;
tunnel. To most people who live and work there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.560
it all blends into the background. But one&nbsp;
piece of street art isn't so easy to ignore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.040
since unlike most graffiti, this one is animate.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:29.520
John Freeman, an unassuming man in his mid&nbsp;
twenties, was out late one night in April of 2004.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:34.800
It was a Saturday, so he'd let loose a little more&nbsp;
than he originally intended and he was starting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.920
feel the effects so he went out back for some&nbsp;
fresh air. This wasn’t the most popular bar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:44.960
in the city, so when he opened up the door and&nbsp;
stepped out, he found himself completely alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:50.560
Despite that fact, he couldn't shake the sense&nbsp;
that he was being watched. He looked around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:56.400
squinting and trying his best to focus his eyes.
Just then John thought he spotted someone behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.880
the dumpster, so he decided to investigate.&nbsp;
When he looked behind the dumpster, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:06.240
was no one to be found though. Instead, painted&nbsp;
on the wall was a piece of street art. It was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:13.120
strange piece of art too. A half-human, half-owl&nbsp;
creature, rendered in highly realistic detail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:18.880
The figure was crouched over, and its eyes faced&nbsp;
outwards. Eyes that looked far too real - like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:24.640
they were staring at him right out of the wall,&nbsp;
following him as he swayed from side to side.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:31.120
There was something so wrong about it. Its intense&nbsp;
gaze, its sharp beak, its grasping claws. How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:36.160
could anyone paint something like this? It looked&nbsp;
like it could crawl right out of the bricks.&nbsp;

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:42.080
Something about the painting made him nervous,&nbsp;
but he couldn't look away. It was drawing him in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.760
Beckoning him. There was something&nbsp;
about it that John just couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:51.680
resist. He stepped closer, until he was&nbsp;
close enough to reach out and touch it.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:57.200
What John didn’t know was that he was also&nbsp;
close enough for it to reach out and touch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.600
him. Before he could react he saw the&nbsp;
painting come to life. The feathers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:09.120
ruffle. The hands prepare to grab. The beak&nbsp;
opens up. For John, it was already too late.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:14.000
From inside the bar, the other patrons heard&nbsp;
a loud scream from the alley. The bartender&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:19.040
ran outside to see what was going on, but&nbsp;
when he got there, all he found was a blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:25.840
stain on the concrete. There was no sign of John&nbsp;
Freeman, and there was no graffiti on the wall.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.800
This is just one of the dozens of recorded&nbsp;
attacks attributed to what the SCP Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:38.240
has designated SCP-1155, or the Predatory&nbsp;
Street Art. It can appear on any wall in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:43.840
any major metropolitan area, but it prefers&nbsp;
ones that are out of the way and isolated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:50.000
such as back alleys, lonely parking structures,&nbsp;
subway tunnels, and underpasses. The creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.040
depicted in the street art has the long,&nbsp;
sinewy arms of a human, with the head and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:02.400
feathery body of a large owl, and though its pose&nbsp;
changes often, it's always facing out… watching.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:08.880
The Foundation is no stranger to deadly&nbsp;
works of art. Such as the infamous SCP - 173,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:14.800
the living sculpture who’ll snap your neck&nbsp;
if you stop looking at it. Or SCP-1074,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:18.880
the abstract art capable of psychologically&nbsp;
enthralling the viewer and leading them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:26.240
into a state of catatonia if exposed for too&nbsp;
long. However, what sets SCP - 1155 apart from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:32.400
these other two is the fact that it’s incredibly&nbsp;
hard to contain and its methods are particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.680
violent and gruesome. But more on that later.
If you spot this odd painting while you're out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:42.720
walking with friends, you probably wouldn’t&nbsp;
give it a second thought. But if you're alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:49.520
it’s a different story. This SCP has an almost&nbsp;
hypnotic effect when viewed by a lone person. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:55.520
you spot it, you'll be overwhelmed by curiosity,&nbsp;
and you'll feel compelled to get a closer look.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:00.560
You can potentially resist this hypnotic effect&nbsp;
with some effort, especially if you're aware of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:07.120
its anomalous properties - meaning knowledge&nbsp;
really is power when it comes to SCP - 1155.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:13.760
Once you get within 2 meters of SCP-1155 though,&nbsp;
the creature will jump out of the wall and strike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:17.760
with attacks usually following the&nbsp;
same pattern. First, the victim will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:22.800
be restrained to prevent struggle or escape,&nbsp;
the eyes and tongue will then be removed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.360
followed by the amputation of the hands and&nbsp;
feet. The victim will then be disemboweled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:34.240
and the intestines and stomach removed. All in as&nbsp;
little as 6 seconds. But not all of these attacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:39.760
result in death. Some have actually managed to&nbsp;
survive attacks by the Predatory Street Art,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:45.680
with those lucky few being eligible for Class&nbsp;
A amnestic therapy, courtesy of the Foundation

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:51.200
In most cases though, there’s no survivors&nbsp;
to find and often not even a body at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:58.080
Once SCP-1155 has begun its attack and if it&nbsp;
remains unspotted, it will snatch up its prey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:03.520
and vanish before appearing on a wall somewhere&nbsp;
else. The Foundation has made attempts to discover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:08.720
where the bodies end up by equipping D-class&nbsp;
test subjects with GPS trackers, but the results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:14.320
of these tests have been inconclusive. Recorded&nbsp;
relocation events have covered distances as small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:22.400
as 15 meters, or as far as 800 kilometers, but the&nbsp;
true range of this creature's movement is unknown.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:29.200
If caught in the act of feeding though, SCP-1155&nbsp;
will disappear, leaving its victim behind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:35.200
Usually in these cases, the victim will bleed&nbsp;
out and die, but some have survived. Two such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:39.920
survivors were D-class personnel, who were used&nbsp;
for tests to see what happened when the attacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:45.120
were interrupted. Both were incoherent&nbsp;
and badly wounded following the attacks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:50.720
Their eyes were gone, and one of them had&nbsp;
also had his tongue, hands, and feet removed.

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:54.800
Though neither could adequately communicate&nbsp;
what had happened to them, the one who still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.800 --> 00:06:00.480
had a tongue claimed to still be able to see, in&nbsp;
spite of his missing eyes, and that he was still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:06.720
looking through his stolen eyes. He described&nbsp;
what he could see: A kind of grisly pantry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:14.000
where it appeared SCP-1155 had stashed the remains&nbsp;
of its previous victims, perhaps to feed on later.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:18.960
This D-Class managed to escape from on-site&nbsp;
quarters during an unrelated containment breach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:24.800
running back to where he'd been attacked by&nbsp;
SCP-1155. He was pursued by law enforcement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:29.440
who had been told by the SCP Foundation that&nbsp;
he was an escaped mental patient suffering from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:34.560
serious delusions. The police chased him for&nbsp;
several blocks before he disappeared down an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:40.160
alleyway. Officers at the scene reported hearing&nbsp;
a scream, but when they got to where it came from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:45.680
there was no sign of the D-class. Just&nbsp;
a dead end alley with a blank wall.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:48.400
The other D-class, the one with no tongue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:53.360
was successfully relocated to an undisclosed&nbsp;
location. While the patient was being moved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:59.280
though, Foundation surveillance noticed an&nbsp;
increased level of movement from SCP-1155.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:04.800
The painting was appearing and disappearing,&nbsp;
and in each relocation its posture suggested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:10.000
hunting and tracking behaviour - as if&nbsp;
it was pursuing the one who got away.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:13.200
The places it was manifesting&nbsp;
also became more and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:18.160
public. At one point it appeared on the side&nbsp;
of a building right in the middle of town,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:23.040
though it was too high up to be reached by any&nbsp;
of the hundreds of witnesses. While all of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:29.040
was happening, the D-class being held for medical&nbsp;
treatment was becoming increasingly distressed.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:33.600
The Foundation theorized that the entity&nbsp;
may have been frustrated at losing its prey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:37.280
and that it would likely continue to&nbsp;
relocate in and out of highly visible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:42.880
areas until the D-class was returned to it.&nbsp;
Because of this, the executive decision was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:49.360
made to take the D-Class to the outskirts of&nbsp;
the city. SCP-1155 manifested in the area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:54.080
and the team left both it&nbsp;
and the D-class unobserved.

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:57.040
Both the creature and the&nbsp;
D-class soon disappeared,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:01.760
and SCP-1155 resumed its more&nbsp;
manageable hunting behaviour.

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:08.800
Due to this SCP's ability to jump from place to&nbsp;
place, it has proven difficult if not impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:14.880
to contain, and has a well-earned Keter Class&nbsp;
designation as a result. At first, the Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:20.640
tried physically removing the wall on which the&nbsp;
SCP had manifested, but this only caused it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:26.160
relocate. A similar result happened following&nbsp;
attempts to paint over or damage the painting.

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:30.960
Current containment procedures involve closely&nbsp;
monitoring the disused shopping mall lot where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:36.800
SCP-1155 currently seems to 'live', for&nbsp;
lack of a better term. The mall has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:41.280
marked as 'condemned', and Foundation&nbsp;
agents continually monitor the area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:46.720
posing as security guards to dissuade any&nbsp;
civilian traffic from entering the danger zone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:52.160
Mobile Task Force Pi-1 - nicknamed “The City&nbsp;
Slickers” - tried to obscure any surface on which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:57.040
the creature appeared, but this didn’t end well.
First, the team leader ordered that a vending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:01.680
machine be placed in front of the graffiti while&nbsp;
it was located in an alley that transients were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:07.200
known to frequent. This was supposed to only&nbsp;
be a temporary stop-gap, intended to stop any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:13.840
unsuspecting person from falling under SCP-1155's&nbsp;
hypnotic spell. In time, a proper containment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:19.120
zone would be established, with more agents&nbsp;
forming a security perimeter around the area.

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:23.680
But, by the time the extra resources&nbsp;
arrived and the vending machine was moved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:28.560
the painting had disappeared. It had&nbsp;
responded to the obstruction by relocating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:34.720
and reappeared on a wall at a nearby children's&nbsp;
playground. The Foundation quickly mobilized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:39.600
interrupting SCP-1155 in the middle of&nbsp;
an attack in order to trigger another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:45.120
relocation - but sadly, several lives were&nbsp;
already lost by the time they arrived.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:50.560
The entire ordeal was a tragic and&nbsp;
costly mistake on the Foundation’s part.

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:57.760
It seems that when SCP-1155 is obstructed, it&nbsp;
gets angry. Instances where it relocated after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:03.200
being obstructed more often than not resulted in&nbsp;
it moving to a much more public place that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:08.800
be harder to contain for the Foundation. While&nbsp;
it previously appeared in low traffic areas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:14.160
it now seemed emboldened following containment&nbsp;
attempts and was readily appearing in public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:19.120
spaces. It was argued that all of humanity&nbsp;
would be better off allowing a few people to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:26.160
be taken by SCP-1155 every couple of years, than&nbsp;
risk it ending up in a heavily populated area&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:30.640
where it could endanger who knows how&nbsp;
many people before it was noticed.

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:34.800
The Foundation continued to research&nbsp;
possible containment procedures though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:39.520
and eventually figured out the perfect minimum&nbsp;
distance at which they could create a barrier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:44.160
so that it wouldn't be noticed by the&nbsp;
SCP itself. This was one of their more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:50.640
expensive containment protocols, as it required&nbsp;
purchasing an entire mall, only to shut it down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:55.520
and condemn it under the pretense of there&nbsp;
being a dangerous sinkhole underneath it.

00:10:55.520 --> 00:11:02.000
The expense seems to have been worth it though,&nbsp;
as SCP-1155 is currently still inside the parking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:06.880
structure connected to the mall. The mall and&nbsp;
the parking structure are under round-the-clock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:12.720
surveillance via motion capture security cameras&nbsp;
and while there have been no other relocations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:17.920
there’s no telling what might trigger&nbsp;
one again. So if you live in a big city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:23.040
and you find yourself walking alone at&nbsp;
night, keep an eye on the street art. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:27.840
you spot a painting of half-human, half-owl&nbsp;
that you could swear wasn't there before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:33.440
resist the urge to check it out,&nbsp;
and just cross the street instead.

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:39.920
Now go check out another tale of a man eating&nbsp;
monster, “SCP - 082 - Fernand the Cannibal”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:47.600
or “SCP - 3001 - Red Reality” for one of the&nbsp;
scariest anomalous locations known to man!

